The directory is empty, so I'll show you the implementation pattern directly. Here's how to add global error handling with `IExceptionHandler`:

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourProject.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Let the default handler produce a safe 500

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title, // Never use exception.Message — it may leak internals
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
// Add these services
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

var app = builder.Build();

// Add middleware BEFORE endpoint routing
app.UseExceptionHandler();  // Catches exceptions, uses IExceptionHandler chain
app.UseStatusCodePages();   // Converts bare status codes to Problem Details
```

**How it works:**

- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the default handler to return RFC 7807 JSON for unhandled exceptions (returns 500 with no sensitive details).
- Your custom `ApiExceptionHandler` runs first. If it recognizes the exception type, it writes the mapped Problem Details response. If not, it returns `false` and the built-in handler produces a generic 500.
- `UseStatusCodePages()` ensures non-exception error responses (e.g., manually setting 404) also get Problem Details formatting.

**Example response for `KeyNotFoundException`:**

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Not Found",
  "instance": "/api/products/999"
}
```

No try-catch needed in any endpoint — just throw the appropriate exception from your service layer.
